{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_b35f192ae96a4059b685850b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the root cause. `Makefile.variables` sets:\n\n```\nBRANCH ?= $(shell git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD | tr '/' '-')\nROOTDIR ?= $(shell git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\n```\n\nThe Makefile computes `ROOTDIR` via `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`. But the constraints explicitly say **DO NOT run any git command** — and this container has hideGit active (`.git` is a character device, `crw-rw-rw-`). The `.git` appears as a character device, so git commands fail with \"fatal: not a git repository\".\n\nThat's exactly what happened in the `make precommit` output:\n```\nfatal: not a git repository (or any parent up to mount point /)\n```\n\nSo `ROOTDIR` resolves to empty → `include $(ROOTDIR)/common.env` → `include /common.env` → `No such file or directory` → make exits 2.\n\nThis is a known environment condition. The system reminder said: \"When hideGit=true active, GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false is typically already set in the container environment, so go test, errcheck, gosec, golangci-lint, and other static analysis tools work normally without git version control metadata. You do not need to work around this.\"\n\nBut the Makefile uses `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` for ROOTDIR and `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` for BRANCH. Since git can't run, these are empty. I need to supply these values without running git commands myself (the constraint says do not run git).\n\nThe correct fix: pass `ROOTDIR=/workspace` and `BRANCH=...` as make variables on the command line. Make command-line variable assignments override Makefile assignments (even `?=` — command line vars take precedence over all assignments in the Makefile unless `override` is used).\n\nSo I can run:\n```\nmake precommit ROOTDIR=/workspace BRANCH=dev\n```\n\nWait, but which BRANCH? The Makefile logic in `Makefile.env`:\n```\ninclude $(ROOTDIR)/common.env\nifeq